* Channel subsystem test 2 -- the initial-status-interruption facility.
*
* THE MOST IMPORTANT TEST IN THIS DIRECTORY, because it is the only one
* aimed at a SEMANTIC gap rather than a field layout.
*
* THE PROBLEM IT SETTLES
* ----------------------
* S/370 SIO sets a condition code SYNCHRONOUSLY: cc=1 means "a CSW has
* been stored, look at it now".  DMKIOS relies on that about a dozen
* times --
*
*      988   BC    4,IOSCC1       BRANCH IF CSW STORED
*     1017   BC    4,TIOCC1       CC = 1    CSW STORED
*     1029   BC    4,HIOCC1       CC = 1 CSW STORED
*
* -- and SSCH gives none of it.  SSCH queues the request and returns.  No
* field mapping fixes that; it is a different contract, and it is the top
* technical risk in the whole conversion.
*
* IBM added a facility for exactly this: initial-status interruption,
* requested by ORB5_I, flag5 bit X'20'.  With it set the subchannel
* reports status as soon as the channel program starts and sets SCSW1_Z,
* the ZERO CONDITION CODE flag -- the substitute for SIO's synchronous
* cc.  The full mapping is the DEFERRED CONDITION CODE in SCSW byte 0
* bits 6-7, which a program reads instead of the cc SIO would have set.
*
* AN EARLIER VERSION OF THE PROJECT DOCS SAID HERCULES CANNOT PROTOTYPE
* THIS.  That was wrong, and this test proves the retraction.  Hercules's
* release notes list "I/O initial status interruption" under version 1.39,
* 24 November 1999.  Hyperion 4.x implements it with PoO citations, and
* channel.c's AIPSX() builds all of "Figure 16-5, the Deferred-Condition-
* Code Meaning for Status-Pending Subchannel".
*
* WHAT THE ANSWERS MEAN
* ---------------------
*   006007   pass.  SCSW1_Z set, intermediate status, deferred cc 0, and
*            the write completed CE|DE.  CP's SIO contract has a working
*            ESA/390 equivalent on the emulator this project runs on.
*
*   000C11   THE INTERESTING FAILURE.  Status arrived but SCSW1_Z was
*            clear, so this build does not implement ISI.  Not a problem
*            with the approach -- 4.x demonstrably has it -- but the lab
*            must move to 4.9.1 before the I/O conversion is developed
*            against it.  Adrian is already there.  Cheap to learn here
*            rather than at M1.
*   000C12   status arrived, Z set, but not flagged intermediate.
*   000C13   deferred condition code was not 0.  Read IRB+0 by hand.
*   000C05   no status at all within 200000 polls.
*   000C03   SSCH itself rejected the ORB -- suspect flag5 X'A0'.
*   000C14   primary status never arrived; the write did not finish.
*   000C08   device status not CE|DE.       000C09  subchannel status set.
*   000C01   STSCH cc/=0.   000C02  MSCH cc/=0.   000C04, 000C06  TSCH cc=3.
*   000C07   second poll exhausted.
*   000DED   program check -- read the old PSW at X'150' for the code.
*
* HOW TO RUN.  Exactly as the other tests: this file as hercules.rc in a
* directory with herc.conf, and nothing else.  To run it after another
* test, EXIT Hercules first, or stop / sysclear / script hercules.rc --
* stop and restart alone re-run whatever is already in storage, which
* looks exactly like a pass.
*
* WHAT IT DOES NOT PROVE.  It polls TSCH rather than taking an I/O
* interruption, so it establishes the STATUS mechanism, not interruption
* delivery.  Status pending is a subchannel condition and TSCH clears it
* whether or not interrupts are enabled, so the SCSW contents are the
* same either way -- but a test with a real I/O new PSW and handler
* proves more, and should come before CP relies on the interrupt path.
*
* Built by build/ssch2.py.  Same device, same config and the same
* MSCH-before-SSCH prerequisite as test 5 -- see that file for why.
*
* KEY BYTES
*   ORB flag5 at 002155 = A0   F=format-1 | I=initial status
*   IRB       at 0021A4
*     IRB+0 bits X'03' deferred condition code   want 00
*     IRB+1 bit  X'04' SCSW1_Z                   want set
*     IRB+3 bit  X'08' intermediate status       want set
*     IRB+3 bit  X'04' primary status
*     IRB+8/+9         device / subchannel status want 0C / 00
*
r 0=0008000000002000
